e here which is  that you can be  for doing the right things but not just  not doing them now just waiting just  being a bit pragmatic and waiting and  delaying until things get a bit cheaper  or whatever it may be until the current  crisis is over and of course if you have  so much of that delaying going on you  get to the stage where actually you're  not responding to climate change with  the speed that science says is necessary  so that would be my worry i don't think  any conservative leader is going to  publicly abandon uh decarbonization for  the reason that [ __ ] just elaborated  let alone the pressure from business but  you know as someone some people have  said delay is the new denial  and richard the uk of course still  retains its presidency of cop 26 until  it hands over to egypt in november  internationally is boris johnson's  ability to lead and put pressure on  other countries to meet their  commitments damaged at all if he's under  pressure at home  well i think it is because he just has  to spend so much attention on these  domestic issues um and of course you  know this is a crucial year for all the  reasons that we were hearing uh earlier  and it would be lovely to think that  boris johnson and the egyptian  leadership were going to work together  towards cop 27 on a joint agenda each  using their kind of influence on the  countries uh with which they have  influence but you know the way things  stand at the moment you have to think  that's not going to be terribly likely  and then if we do have a new  conservative leader a new prime minister  the question is then will they want  to spend large chunks of the year  internationally on climate change or  will they think well that was with the  johnson era and not particularly bother  and cece do you think that highlights  then the problems associated with  short-term democratic governments and  potentially dispensable leaders when  you're dealing with long-term emergency  and crises like the climate one  so i'm i'm not a political expert um but  i do i do agree with richard um here  that climate change is a very urgent  problem um and we need action now i  think the question is really how how do  we actually um kind of deal with the  issue with the systems that we have in  place right now whether it's you know  what kind of it depends you know  whatever the political or economic  system is it is working with what we  have right now but i really do i want to  go back to my point of i think  regardless of the form of government or  political system i do think public  opinion matters as long as we can keep  people interested pushing for the issue  um  there will be policy changes okay let's  turn to our second topic of the evening  and the argentinian capital was plunged  into darkness last night an historic  heatwave with temperatures hitting 45  degrees led to the power grid being  overwhelmed now also this week news that  last year saw the hottest ocean  temperatures in recorded history for the  sixth successive year  both signs of the future how to deal  with the challenges is the big question  and so cc argentina the hottest place in  the world  this week a lot of concern about a trend  of rising temperatures so what should be  the focus now is all we can do is think  about adaptation to that new normal  yeah so adaptation really has to be on  top of the agenda and we know that um  there will be a lot more extreme weather  events such as these i'm experiencing um  in in argentina right now um so we need  to think about how do we establish early  warning systems how do we train people  and inform you know  ask people to um  to know you know what they're gonna do  and also ask our city governments to  prepare our electricity systems and  companies as well how do we have enough  uh measures in place so that we can  adapt um properly to these more  frequent events and richard are you  confident that enough can be done within  adaptation to deal with the rising  temperatures and prevent the risk of  future blackouts  it certainly can be done but it costs  and of course as climate change  progresses and we see more and more  impacts the cost of adapting things will  will increase so we're not terribly sure  exactly what the cause of this um  blackout in buenos aires is but it does  seem to be that part of it has been due  to the extra electricity demand for  cooling which has put pressure on the  grid which then couldn't handle it so  ideally the electricity companies and  the authorities ought to be  strengthening the grid making it more  robust but of course if you've then got  other climate impacts coming down the  track that may sort of outstrip the  speed at which you can make the grid  more robust and it's not just heat waves  we've seen other types of climate impact  climate link should we say extreme  weather events that including in the uk  where you know we've had storms in  cumbria for example several winters that  have been uh show where climate change  has been shown to be one of the drivers  which has caused power cut  sometimes to essential infrastructure  like hospitals we've seen wildfires in  california and so on so it's some  there's an irony here in that several  opponents of decarbonization sometimes  argue that well if the world switches to  renewables we're going to see lots of  power cuts actually here is the the  world responding to climate change the  natural world responding to our  greenhouse gas emissions and it's that  that's causing us blackouts and  associates one thing uh having countries  experiencing  higher temperatures it's a whole  different challenge when you talk about  oceans as well and it's a really serious  issue warmer seas isn't it because that  means rising sea levels and more severe  storms as well so is there anything you  can do about it  well yeah the top thing is that we need  to reduce um emissions right so um if we  start producing more emissions then  we're essentially helping the slow down  the warming up speed of the oceans um i  i do think and also like because um  warming of the oceans creates more  hurricanes and flooding in coastal areas  so we we need to look into adapting in a  sense of building flood defenses and  helping protect these coastal  communities because they're going to be  very very vulnerable  okay  an issue that i'm sure we'll return to  many times but um cece who and richard  black we're out of time but really good  to see both thanks very much indeed  and that's everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show i'll be joined by  chidi oti obihara from the cop 26 action  plan group and former energy and climate  secretary amber rudd thanks for watching  i'll see you next time  [Music]  you
 

